From 4cc296f6eec500259507d657c33292e785ad0bb7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Stew Forster <stew675@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 9 Nov 2026 18:12:11 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] wip: apu-expert-pool -- the UMA split takes ONE snapshot of
 free system memory, so the 75 % VRAM and 10 % pool shares are shares of the
 same figure

The percentages were already of the FULL free figure (not of a remainder), but they were
read at two different instants: measured on halo, the advisory's 75 % came from
MemAvailable = 118 870 MiB while the arena's came from 102 190 MiB, i.e. the two shares did
not add up to 85 % of anything.  Pool's 10 % would have been a third instant.

Now the loader takes the snapshot once (it runs first in the real load, before the model
mapping is populated), hands it to the backend through a new
ggml_backend_dev_moe_cache_set_uma_free() hook, and the cache uses it for BOTH shares and the
slab cap.  Measured after: advisory "~89542.4 MiB = 75%" and arena "budget 89542 MiB/device"
are the same figure; with 120 GiB free the split is VRAM 90 + pool 12 + 18 left to the OS.

Also fixes a link-time landmine found doing this: the public setter must live OUTSIDE
moe-expert-cache.cu's anonymous namespace.  Inside it the symbol has internal linkage, the
compiler drops it, and the binary dies at startup with `libggml-hip.so.0: undefined symbol:
moe_cache_set_uma_free_bytes` -- which is exactly what happened on the first attempt.

Note: the same anonymous-namespace rule caught us once already in this campaign; the new
hook is placed after `} // namespace` with a comment saying why.

Measured: greedy text still byte-identical to `-ncmoe 0`; `MOE_EXPERT_CACHE_UMA=0` disarms the
slab.  The pool's 10 % ceiling is implemented but NOT exercised in this config (the pool is
sized by MOE_HOST_POOL_FRAC of the host set, 3814 MiB < 11.9 GiB, so the ceiling does not
bind) -- it needs the >RAM arm to prove.
